# The national unit for japan. Items take n=2; the world items reviewed here change by level.
# Dates are in engine/notation/chronicle.ts with their sources.
lesson history-g3-japan-our-country-long-ago v=1 format=teach grade=3 unit=9 subject=history nation=japan levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "Tools at home, then and now"
  goal "Put changes in Japan's homes and towns in order, and say how a new tool changed a family's day."

  look {
    scene 36x9 {
      timeline line from=1600 to=2000 step=50 years=[1657, 1800, 1953] labels=["Great Fire of Edo", "Survey of Japan began", "Television broadcasts in Japan"] at=canvas(0, 0)
    }
    say """
      Long ago rice was cooked on a kamado, a clay stove fed with wood, and someone had to watch it all the time. In 1953 television broadcasts began in Japan. In 1955 the first automatic electric rice cooker went on sale, and rice could cook itself. Edo had burned in 1657 and was built again, and Ino Tadataka began to map the whole coast in 1800.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked history.n3-what n=2
    }
    show history.n3-order n=2 k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show history.n3-between n=2 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.n3-century n=2 k=0 hard-k=1
    show history.bc-ad-order k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.across-year-one k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.which-century k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=4
    show history.n3-order n=2 k=2 easy-k=1 hard-k=0
    level medium hard {
      show history.n3-what n=2
    }
    level easy {
      show history.n3-century n=2 k=1
    }
    level hard {
      show history.bc-ad-order k=5
    }
  }
  story {
    show history.n3-between n=2 k=1 hard-k=0
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show history.n3-why n=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show history.n3-find-out n=2
  }
  remember {
    say "Every country's story goes back further than its writing. Where there is no writing, the ground and the buildings tell it."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is to put the grade's methods (timelines across year one, centuries, evidence without
    writing) to work on the family's own country. The way in names what the place or thing was;
    the core orders three events of the country, counts the years between two, names a century,
    and reviews three of the grade's timeline questions; the two-star question asks how we know.
    The wrong idea to watch for is that a date in a book is when something began, when it is often
    only the first time it was written down or the oldest thing found. If the child is stuck, put
    the three events on a line you draw together. The three-star task is for you to mark: a small
    piece of finding out with you, near home. Japan's 社会 for grades three and four asks children to study 「古くから残る暮らしにかかわる道具，それらを使っていたころの暮らしの様子」, the old tools of daily life and how people lived with them (the course of study as read in its 2008 edition).
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level a worked example says what the place was, the events come nearly in order, the years counted are the closest pair, and the review questions stay on one side of year one."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the events are fully muddled, the years counted are the longer pair, the century asked has a trap in it, and the review questions cross year one."
  }
}
